    <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>By <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/someone">Jane Doe</a>, <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/other">Sam Roe</a> and Alex Poe</p><h3>Why we rebuilt it</h3><p>Our streaming platform moves billions of events a day through Kafka. This post explains how we rewrote the consumers in Python, cut end-to-end latency in half, and open-sourced the tooling at <a href="https://github.com/Example-OSS/stream-kit">github.com/Example-OSS/stream-kit</a>. The work builds on <a href="https://github.com/apache/kafka/pull/12345">an upstream Kafka change</a> and <a href="https://github.com/confluentinc/confluent-kafka-python.git">confluent-kafka-python</a>.</p><figure><img src="https://cdn-images.example.com/chart.png" alt="latency chart"/><figcaption>Latency before and after</figcaption></figure><p>We also tried <a href="https://github.com/features/actions">GitHub Actions</a> for CI and filed feedback via <a href="https://github.com/orgs/Example-OSS/discussions">our org discussions</a>.</p><pre><code>consumer.poll(timeout=1.0)</code></pre><p>The new pipeline is written in Python and deployed on Kubernetes. More Python details below, plus notes on Kafka partitioning and data pipelines in general.</p><img src="https://medium.com/_/stat?event=post.clientViewed" width="1" height="1" alt="">]]></content:encoded>
